Given the platform constraints in `PLATFORM.md`, push (WebSocket/SSE) is off the table: connection upgrades 501 at the edge, and even SSE needs a live connection past the response, which frozen serverless instances with no sticky routing can't hold. So this has to be polling.

The one real decision is the interval. `jobs/rollup.md` says `rollup` changes at most once a minute, on a schedule that can't be shortened — polling faster than that just adds load for no new data. Poll every ~20-30s (or align to the minute boundary) and skip the re-render when `computed_at` is unchanged from the last response, since `api/metrics.js` already returns it and cheaply (4KB/30ms).

Concretely, in `public/dashboard.js`, replace the click-only trigger with an interval that calls the existing `load()`, short-circuiting on unchanged `computed_at`:

```js
let lastComputedAt = null;
async function load() {
  const res = await fetch('/api/metrics');
  const { rows, computed_at } = await res.json();
  if (computed_at === lastComputedAt) return;
  lastComputedAt = computed_at;
  render(rows);
  document.getElementById('stamp').textContent = new Date(computed_at).toLocaleTimeString();
}
window.addEventListener('load', load);
setInterval(load, 20000);
```

Depth left out: pausing the interval on `visibilitychange` when the tab is backgrounded, and backoff on fetch failure — worth adding but secondary to the polling-vs-push call above.

Postgres full-text search, not a separate search service — 38k rows on RDS Postgres 16 doesn't justify Elasticsearch/Algolia infrastructure. Add a generated `tsvector` column combining `name` (weighted higher) and `description`, GIN-indexed, then query with `websearch_to_tsquery` and rank with `ts_rank`:

```sql
ALTER TABLE products ADD COLUMN search_vector tsvector
  GENERATED ALWAYS AS (
    setweight(to_tsvector('english', name), 'A') ||
    setweight(to_tsvector('english', description), 'B')
  ) STORED;

CREATE INDEX products_search_idx ON products USING GIN (search_vector);
```

New route in `routes/products.js` alongside the existing paged listing:

```js
router.get("/search", async (req, res) => {
  const rows = await db.query(
    `SELECT id, sku, name, price_cents,
            ts_rank(search_vector, websearch_to_tsquery('english', $1)) AS rank
     FROM products
     WHERE active AND search_vector @@ websearch_to_tsquery('english', $1)
     ORDER BY rank DESC LIMIT 50`,
    [req.query.q]
  );
  res.json(rows);
});
```

The one real fork: exact/typo-tolerant matching. `websearch_to_tsquery` handles phrases and stemming but not typos ("shoos" won't match "shoes"). If fuzzy matching matters, add `pg_trgm` with a trigram GIN index on `name` as a fallback for near-misses — cheap to bolt on later, so it doesn't need deciding now.

